In C programming, there are several ways to terminate a program. Here are some common methods:

1. Normal termination: A program can be terminated normally by reaching the `main` function's return statement. This is the preferred way to terminate a program when it has finished executing all the instructions. For example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here
    printf("Program finished executing.\n");
    return 0; // normal termination
}
```

2. Abnormal termination: A program can be terminated abnormally using the `exit()` function. This function causes the immediate termination of the program, and it returns a status code to the operating system. For example:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here
    if (some_condition) {
        printf("Condition met, terminating program.\n");
        exit(1); // abnormal termination
    }
    // More code here
    return 0; // normal termination
}
```

3. Termination due to errors: If there is an error in the program, such as a segmentation fault or a divide-by-zero error, the program may be terminated by the operating system. In this case, the program will return a non-zero status code to the operating system.

It is important to note that abnormal termination can lead to data corruption and other issues, so it should be used only when necessary. Normal termination is the preferred way to ensure that all resources are properly released and that the program has completed its intended functionality.
